notes.body:
A proposition is a statement that is either true or false, never both. `p AND q` is true only when both are true; `p OR q` is true when at least one is true; `NOT p` flips the value. Worked example: evaluate `(p AND q) OR (NOT r)` at p=T, q=F, r=T. First `p AND q` = T AND F = F. Then `NOT r` = NOT T = F. Finally `F OR F` = **F**. So the whole expression is **F**. Work inside-out: parentheses first, then NOT, then AND, then OR — the same precedence habit that prevents bugs in code conditions.

